  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 120 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nMonday continues the significant cold air advection pattern with\nlots of low cloud cover and perhaps a early day round of light\nrain or drizzle to help add to the chill. Monday's highs should be\nthe lowest of the week with the maxes mainly in the 50s to low\n60s. The cloud cover will minimize nighttime cooling, and the\nTuesday morning lows are expected to be comparable to the Monday\nlows. \n\nA shortwave upper ridge moving in the zonal pattern over TX brings\na temporary end to rain chances but doesn't appear to have enough\nstrength or persistent to scour out the low level moisture. So we\nmay get some partly cloudy skies late Monday into early Tuesday,\nbut mostly cloudy skies should continue provide cooling through\nmost of the work week. South winds return mid-week but also bring\nback a thicker layer of low clouds and a slight effect of\noverrunning as falling heights aloft upstream from TX translate to\nweak coastal troughing over the TX coast. Temperatures should\nmoderate back to near seasonal norms, but rain chances will return\nmainly in the form of streamer convection. By Friday, the latest \ndeterministic runs depict hints of embedded shortwave activity in \nthe, by then, SW flow aloft pattern, which could offer the chance \nfor upscaling convection.",
